Every time I used an LLM, the same question kept nagging:
how does it actually work?

Not "it predicts the next word." The real version.
What each layer does. Why this architecture won.

Reading wasn't getting me there. So I built it.

10 components. From scratch. NumPy throughout,
PyTorch only at the end.

Perceptron → MLP → Backprop → Optimization → Regularization
→ CNNs → BatchNorm & ResNets → RNNs → Attention → Transformer

Each one answered what the previous one couldn't.
Backprop fixed what perceptrons couldn't learn.
ResNets fixed what depth broke.
Attention fixed what RNNs forgot.

Strip away the hype — an LLM is math arranged into architecture, filled with data, forged by compute, tempered by training.

By post 10, a small GPT trained on Shakespeare —
every line of it traceable to an earlier post.

